+\section{Structural-encoding feasibility}
+\label{sec:structural-feasibility}
+
+The compiler case study above evaluates one production implementation. A
+separate, predeclared campaign asked whether exact structural encodings of
+whole-program schedule/address choices could be sampled and then used in further experiments.
+This feasibility question matters because rank encodings and structural
+representations require a well-defined finite domain of legal completions.
+The campaign's result is inconclusive: its public sampler did not attain the
+specified minimum coverage, so the dependent comparisons were not measured.
+
+\subsection{Sampling design and finite checks}
+The locked domain fixes the issue-time fields by operation ID and then the
+selected scratch-address fields by producer ID, with least-significant-bit-first
+coordinates. Two distinct streams sampled each public program. Raw-bit sampling
+draws uniform fixed-width bit strings and decodes them; strings may be invalid
+or may identify dead ends. Option-path sampling chooses uniformly among the
+available physical choices at each decision, stopping at a dead end. These are
+different distributions, and neither is assumed to be uniform over feasible
+schedules. The domain uses issue cycles from zero through one less than the
+program horizon and all legal physical scratch bases. The incumbent is first in
+the option order when available, followed by remaining choices in ascending
+order. Each stream requested 10,000 attempts per program,
+with a 60-second per-program, per-stream limit and no invisible retries.
+Coverage is the union of distinct completed compilations across both streams;
+the gate requires at least 100 per program. This threshold is a sampling
+criterion, not an estimate of the total feasible set.
+
+Finite fixture checks covered 12 locked programs and all four declared codecs.
+Across those fixtures, 24 small code universes were exhausted and 564
+round-trip checks were performed. These denominators do not imply that every
+codec's complete code universe was exhausted on every fixture. The checks found
+no round-trip failures or finite-domain discrepancies for the tested objects.
+
+\subsection{Public coverage and disposition}
+Table~\ref{tab:phase2-coverage} reports completed draws by stream and the
+distinct union across streams. Stream counts may include repeated compilations;
+the union deduplicates them. These are not case-execution counts.
+All raw-bit streams produced zero completions. The campaign retained 160,000
+attempts in 16 streams, 819 completed draws, and 1,356 case checks; it observed
+zero validator discrepancies. Four programs fell below the 100-completion
+threshold.
+
